[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: What 'parent' means

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[NARRATIVE]
UID: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-PARENT-CHILD
TITLE: What 'parent' means
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
WIDGET: rows
PLACE: screen
STATEMENT: >>>
strictdoc's trace direction, this repo's reading of it as 'depends on', and the closure walk.

- In strictdoc, writing 'Parent: B' on node A means A traces to B, and B's child link is derived. Role names are labels only. All eight roles here are Parent links; the reverse names are never used, and the JSON export drops them.
> strictdoc: "TYPE: Parent, VALUE: B" on A = A traces to B; B's child link derived; ROLE/REVERSE_ROLE are labels; all 8 roles are Parent, 0 reverse uses, JSON export drops REVERSE_ROLE [WORKS]
- This repo reads 'parent' as 'what I depend on', and the closure walk — a node plus everything it depends on, transitively — treats a probe, a superseding decision and a backlog register all as dependencies alike.
> our overload: parent = "what I depend on"; closure (all Parent edges any role + all PARENT_FP) weighs SPIKE, successor and register alike [UNADDRESSED]
- No Child relation is declared or used. The readiness query only follows Parent links, so a future Child link would never count as a dependency.
> no Child relation declared, 0 uses; readiness filters TYPE == Parent so a future Child is never a dependency [WORKS]
- The one wanted use of a Child link — tying code to its layer through boundary nodes in the spec — has no boundary node and no layer relation to hang on.
[LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-GAP-SEMANTICS-LIVE-IN-TOOLS-WITH-NO-OWNER]
> wanted Child use: code -> its layer via boundary nodes in docs/spec; no boundary node, no layer relation exists [GAP]
- Because Superseded_By is a Parent link, the successor becomes the retired decision's parent. Whether depending on a superseded decision blocks readiness is undecided.
[LINK: DEC-SUPERSESSION-RULE] [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-DECISION]
> Superseded_By makes the successor the retired node's parent; does a superseded parent block a closure? [OPEN: DEC-SUPERSESSION-RULE]
- Three things the spec fixes: a reader pulls context in along edges rather than reading everything; each plan has one owner; and a rule that lives only in a document is not a rule.
[LINK: DEC-SYSTEM-PURPOSE] [LINK: INV-GUARDS-MUST-EXECUTE] [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-REQUIREMENT] [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-DECISION]
> docs/spec bounds: reader pulls in by edge (DEC-SYSTEM-PURPOSE); one owning identity per plan; a rule must run (INV-GUARDS-MUST-EXECUTE) [WORKS]
<<<
PARENT_FP: >>>
